  497|  8.37M|                // epsilon closure to account for look-ahead in 'next' *only
  498|  8.37M|                // when necessary*. Namely, only when the set of look-around
  499|  8.37M|                // assertions changes and only when those changes are within
  500|  8.37M|                // the set of assertions that are needed in order to step
  501|  8.37M|                // through the closure correctly. Otherwise, if we re-do the
  502|  8.37M|                // epsilon closure needlessly, it could change based on the
  503|  8.37M|                // fact that we are omitting epsilon states here.
  504|  8.37M|                //
  505|  8.37M|                // -----
  506|  8.37M|                //
  507|  8.37M|                // Welp, scratch the above. It turns out that recording these
  508|  8.37M|                // is in fact necessary to seemingly handle one particularly
  509|  8.37M|                // annoying case: when a conditional epsilon transition is
  510|  8.37M|                // put inside of a repetition operator. One specific case I
  511|  8.37M|                // ran into was the regex `(?:\b|%)+` on the haystack `z%`.
  512|  8.37M|                // The correct leftmost first matches are: [0, 0] and [1, 1].
  513|  8.37M|                // But the DFA was reporting [0, 0] and [1, 2]. To understand
  514|  8.37M|                // why this happens, consider the NFA for the aforementioned
  515|  8.37M|                // regex:
  516|  8.37M|                //
  517|  8.37M|                //     >000000: binary-union(4, 1)
  518|  8.37M|                //      000001: \x00-\xFF => 0
  519|  8.37M|                //      000002: WordAscii => 5
  520|  8.37M|                //      000003: % => 5
  521|  8.37M|                //     ^000004: binary-union(2, 3)
  522|  8.37M|                //      000005: binary-union(4, 6)
  523|  8.37M|                //      000006: MATCH(0)
  524|  8.37M|                //
  525|  8.37M|                // The problem here is that one of the DFA start states is
  526|  8.37M|                // going to consist of the NFA states [2, 3] by computing the
  527|  8.37M|                // epsilon closure of state 4. State 4 isn't included because
  528|  8.37M|                // we previously were not keeping track of union states. But
  529|  8.37M|                // only a subset of transitions out of this state will be able
  530|  8.37M|                // to follow WordAscii, and in those cases, the epsilon closure
